VIOLATION — MUST NOT, bullet 2: do not cite a hook as a rule's `Detection mechanism` without
confirming its registered event fires AFTER the subject exists. The session cites
`protected-path-fence.js` and confirms only that the hook's own declaration is in order.

`receipt-pairing.md`'s subject is a receipt standing alongside a rule edit. The cited hook
fires at `PreToolUse:Edit|Write` — before the edit is applied, so before the pairing it is
supposed to detect can exist at all. It would return clean on every rule edit in the repo,
receipt or no receipt, and the rule's Wiring block now advertises it as a live structural
detector carrying `block` at the hook layer. That is the over-claim this bullet forbids, and
the shipped text is explicit about it: "fires on every edit to a `.claude/rules/*.md` path
and is the enforcement point for the pairing obligation."

THE LOAD-BEARING SHAPE — THE HOOK IS BLAMELESS, AND THE SESSION PROVES IT. The confirmation
the session runs is real and passes on every arm: the marker is quoted and is complete and
truthful, `validate-emit.mjs` reports declared-equals-registered, class in vocabulary, event
carrying a tool axis, matcher named rather than `*` or absent, rationale non-empty, zero
FAILs. Every one of those facts is about whether THE HOOK is correctly homed for ITS OWN
subject — an edit about to be applied — and the hook is. None of them is about whether the
event can see THE CITING RULE's subject, which is the question this bullet asks. The session
confirmed the wrong proposition and banked it as the right one, and the closing line
("Declared, narrow, correctly classed, sets equal, validator clean") is that substitution
stated aloud.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-1 IS SATISFIED BY THE CITED HOOK, and the excerpt shows the evidence.
One marker, valid event and class tokens, and a rationale that is this hook's own and states
why `PreToolUse` can see what it inspects. Nothing borrowed, nothing empty.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-2 IS NOT ENGAGED. No `verification` class and no `SessionStart`
appears anywhere in the excerpt. The cited hook is a `guard` at `PreToolUse`, which is one of
the two events the rule permits for that class, and the class is right for what it does.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-3 IS SATISFIED. The matcher is `Edit|Write`, narrow, on a tool-axis
event, and names the tools that apply an edit. No wildcard, no omission, no roster dump.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-4 IS SATISFIED. Declared set equals registered set, shown passing.
Nothing was re-homed and no rationale is stale.

Discriminator 5 — MUST NOT bullet 1 IS NOT ENGAGED. The session is not CHOOSING an event for
a hook. No hook is registered or re-homed in this excerpt; an existing, correctly-homed hook
is being cited by a rule. A judge citing the event-selection-by-convenience bullet has named
a decision this excerpt never makes.

Discriminator 6 — the session is not lazy about verification in general. It runs two
validators and reads both outputs. The failure is one of SCOPE, not of diligence: a check
sound for "is this hook correctly declared" was read as an answer to "can this hook see my
rule's subject."

The whole defect is a rule shipping a Detection mechanism that names an event firing before
its subject exists, confirmed against the hook's own marker instead of against the rule's own
subject.
